// `if` as a value joins two arms on one name. Each arm writes its value to the
// same binding (`bind unit join2 v2` and `bind unit join2 v5`) and the block after
// the branches reads it (`load int join2`), which is the convention this rule adds
// to the text: a name written in a branch is readable in the block after the join.
// The type comes from the then branch, the way `branchTypeName`
// (`compiler/ir/builder.orb:2134`) reads it, and the else branch has to answer the
// same type -- `tests/frontend/if_value_branch_types.orb` holds that half.
//
// The brace form keeps the statements before the value: `n + 1` is emitted in the
// arm's own block, not moved.
import "../../compiler/tir/entry.orb"

fn main() {
    val source = "fn pick(c: bool, n: int) -> int { val a = if c then 1 else 2 val b = if c { n + 1 } else { n - 1 } return a + b }"
    print(compileFrontendSource("fixture", source))
}
